# 3ds Max Technical & Modeling Rules

## 1. Code Standards & Safety
- Write robust, modular, and error-checked MAXScript (`.ms`, `.mcr`) or 3ds Max Python API code.
- Always wrap procedural mesh creation in `undo off` and `redraw off` blocks to maximize performance during heavy iteration.
- Clean up temporary variables and clear selection arrays at the start and end of scripts to avoid memory leaks or viewport clutter.

## 2. Procedural Geometry & Complexity Management
- When building complex structures, break them down into modular components (e.g., base mesh, primary details, secondary paneling, fasteners).
- Explicitly define vertex, edge, and polygon indices when manipulating sub-objects via MeshOps or PolyOps.
- Enforce clean topology principles: favor quad-dominant modeling where applicable, prevent overlapping vertices by using appropriate tolerance thresholds (e.g., `welldist`), and avoid zero-length edges or flipped normals.
- Implement explicit naming conventions for all generated nodes (e.g., `Prefix_ModelName_ID`).

## 3. Modifiers & Hierarchy Stack
- Build modifier stacks logically from bottom to top: Skin/Morph -> Edit Poly/Mesh -> Symmetry/Turbosmooth -> UVW Map.
- Never collapse stacks destructively unless explicitly requested, allowing non-destructive adjustments to parameters like segment counts or noise scales.
- Group related objects using `dummy` helpers or hierarchical parent-child linking to maintain an organized Scene Explorer.
